.TH DECAF 1 "2026-11-23" "decaf" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
decaf \- suspend the system after a while
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B decaf on
.I DURATION
.RB [ \-\-notify ]
.br
.B decaf off
.RB [ \-\-notify ]
.br
.B decaf status
.RB [ \-\-notify ]
.br
.B decaf
.BR \-\-help " | " \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B decaf
suspends the system after a given number of seconds, even if your own programs
try to keep it awake.
It is meant to be scripted: output on stdout is data only, messages go to
stderr, and every outcome has its own exit status.
.PP
A timer is a transient systemd user unit,
.BR app-decaf.service .
While the unit runs, a suspend is scheduled; there is no other state.
Each user has at most one timer, shared by all their sessions.
.PP
.B decaf
is the opposite of
.BR expresso (1),
which keeps the system awake for a while.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI on " DURATION"
Suspend the system in
.I DURATION
seconds.
.I DURATION
is a whole number from 1 to 2147483647, without sign or leading zeros.
Fails if a timer is already on.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-notify
If a timer is already on, also show it as a desktop notification; see
.BR NOTIFICATIONS .
The exit status is the same.
.RE
.TP
.B off
Cancel the timer. Returns once it is fully cancelled.
Fails if no timer is on.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-notify
If no timer is on, also show that as a desktop notification.
The exit status is the same.
.RE
.TP
.B status
Print the timer's state; see
.BR "STATUS OUTPUT" .
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-notify
Also show the state as a desktop notification; see
.BR NOTIFICATIONS .
The output and exit status are the same.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH STATUS OUTPUT
.B status
prints
.IB key = value
lines.
When no timer is on, the only line is
.BR state=off .
When a timer is on, all of these keys are printed, in this order:
.TP
.B state
.BR on .
.TP
.B duration
The
.I DURATION
given to
.BR on .
.TP
.B started
When the timer was set, as a Unix timestamp.
.TP
.B until
When the system suspends, as a Unix timestamp.
.TP
.B remaining
Seconds until then.
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
state=on
duration=1800
started=1759158600
until=1759160400
remaining=1740
.fi
.RE
.SH TIMING AND SUSPEND
The deadline is wall-clock time:
.B on 1800
means in 30 minutes from now, and time spent suspended counts.
.PP
Sixty seconds before the deadline, a "Suspending soon" notification gives you
the chance to run
.BR "decaf off" .
If the system resumes within that last minute, the notification appears at
once. Timers of 60 seconds or less have no warning.
.PP
If the system was already asleep at the deadline (you suspended it earlier and
resumed later), the timer is skipped: it ends when the system resumes, without
suspending it again.
Suspending and resuming before the deadline keeps the timer.
.PP
.SH SLEEP LOCKS
Programs can hold inhibitor locks against sleep; see
.BR systemd-inhibit (1).
.B systemd-inhibit \-\-list
shows them.
At the deadline,
.BR decaf :
.IP \(bu 2
suspends past blocking locks held by your own user, such as a download,
.BR expresso (1),
or a script that runs
.BR systemd-inhibit ;
see
.BR PERMISSIONS ;
.IP \(bu 2
never suspends past a blocking lock held by the system or by another user,
such as a firmware update or a system upgrade.
It does not suspend: it shows "Decaf blocked" with the lock's program and
reason, and the timer ends.
.PP
Locks in
.B delay
mode never stop a suspend.
A lock in
.B block\-weak
mode (systemd 257 or later) blocks every user but its owner, so
.B decaf
suspends past your own with no override and no password.
.PP
If
.B decaf
cannot read the locks, it suspends without overriding any: then any blocking
lock stops it, and the timer ends as failed.
.SH PERMISSIONS
A suspend that no lock blocks needs no password.
Suspending past your own blocking locks is polkit's
.B org.freedesktop.login1.suspend\-ignore\-inhibit
action, which by default asks for an administrator password: a prompt appears
at the deadline.
If nobody answers it within 25 seconds, the system does not suspend and the
timer ends as failed.
.PP
To suspend past them without a password, so that a timer works while nobody is
there, join the
.B decaf
group:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo usermod \-aG decaf "$USER"
.fi
.RE
.PP
The polkit rule installed with
.B decaf
lets members of the group take that action without a password, from an active
local session.
polkit cannot tell which program asks, so membership lets every program you run
suspend past any sleep lock, not only
.BR decaf ;
.B decaf
itself still never suspends past the locks of the system or of other users.
To check that the rule applies to you (exit status 0):
.PP
.RS
.nf
pkcheck \-\-action\-id org.freedesktop.login1.suspend\-ignore\-inhibit \e
    \-\-process $$
.fi
.RE
.PP
Alternatively, have your own programs take
.B block\-weak
locks
.RB ( "systemd\-inhibit \-\-mode=block\-weak" ),
which need no override at all.
But then they stop nothing you run, not even your idle manager's suspend.
.SH NOTIFICATIONS
The timer unit itself sends desktop notifications, whatever started or stopped
it: when the timer is set ("Decaf on"), shortly before the suspend ("Suspending
soon"), when it is skipped or turned off ("Decaf off"), when a lock of the
system or another user blocks the suspend ("Decaf blocked", with the lock; see
.BR "SLEEP LOCKS" ),
and when it fails ("Decaf failed", with where to look in the journal).
There is none when the system suspends.
.PP
.B status \-\-notify
shows the timer's state on request, for example from a keybinding: "Decaf
status: on" with when it suspends and the time left ("Suspending at 15:40 (29m
left)"), or "Decaf status: off" with "No timer is on".
With
.BR \-\-notify ,
.B on
and
.B off
also notify when there is nothing to do, since the timer unit then sends
nothing: "Decaf status: already on" with the running timer, as
.B status
shows it, or "Decaf status: already off" with "No timer is on".
Without it, a toggle such as
.B "decaf off || decaf on 1800"
shows only the timer unit's notification.
Otherwise the commands send no notifications themselves, and
.B on
and
.B off
print nothing on success.
.PP
The titles of the notifications asked for with
.B \-\-notify
say "status:", which tells them apart from the timer unit's own.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
For
.BR status :
a timer is on.
.TP
.B 1
No timer is on
.RB ( status ", " off ).
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: unknown command or option, invalid or missing
.IR DURATION .
.TP
.B 3
A timer is already on
.RB ( on ).
.TP
.B 4
System error: systemd could not be reached, the timer could not be set or
cancelled, or
.B app-decaf.service
exists but was not started by this version of
.BR decaf ,
or a notification asked for with
.B \-\-notify
could not be sent.
.SH EXAMPLES
Suspend in 45 minutes:
.PP
.RS
.nf
decaf on 2700
.fi
.RE
.PP
Toggle a 30-minute timer, for example from a keybinding:
.PP
.RS
.nf
decaf off 2>/dev/null || decaf on 1800
.fi
.RE
.PP
Separate keys to set and cancel a 30-minute timer, each with a notification
even when there is nothing to do, and one to show the state:
.PP
.RS
.nf
decaf on 1800 \-\-notify
decaf off \-\-notify
decaf status \-\-notify >/dev/null
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check for a timer, and read when it fires:
.PP
.RS
.nf
if decaf status >/dev/null; then
    date \-d "@$(decaf status | sed \-n 's/^until=//p')"
fi
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
There is no configuration file; put your preferred durations in a keybinding,
alias or script.
.TP
.I /usr/share/polkit\-1/rules.d/50\-decaf.rules
The polkit rule for the
.B decaf
group; see
.BR PERMISSIONS .
.TP
.I /usr/lib/sysusers.d/decaf.conf
Creates the
.B decaf
group.
.PP
Installed from source, both are under the install prefix, by default
.IR /usr/local .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR expresso (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR systemd-run (1),
.BR systemd-inhibit (1),
.BR notify-send (1),
.BR polkit (8),
.BR pkcheck (1)
.SH BUGS
Report issues at https://github.com/Davi-S/decaf/issues
